/* System Design track (new units). Appended after content-code.js; never reorder (ids depend on order). */
window.CURRICULUM.push(

{t:"Interview Craft & Estimation", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Requirements, SLOs and the Nines",
"Turn a vague ask into functional requirements (what the system does) plus measurable non-functional targets: latency percentiles, availability, durability and freshness. ‘Fast and reliable’ is not a requirement. ‘p99 under 200 ms at 99.9% availability’ is.\n\nEach extra nine costs far more to build and run:\n\n$99.9%   → ~8.8 h down per year (~43 min per month)\n99.99%  → ~53 min down per year (~4.4 min per month)\n99.999% → ~5 min down per year\n\nTrack p99, not the average, because the tail is what unlucky users feel. The SLO is your internal target; the SLA is the contractual promise to customers, usually looser. State freshness and consistency targets explicitly too, such as ‘features at most 5 minutes stale’.",
[["A 99.9% availability SLO allows roughly how much downtime per month?",["About 43 minutes","About 4.4 minutes","About 8.8 hours","About 7 hours"],"0.1% of a 30-day month (43,200 minutes) is about 43 minutes. 4.4 minutes is 99.99%; 8.8 hours is the yearly budget at 99.9%."],
 {type:"match", p:"Match each term to its meaning", pairs:[["SLO","Internal reliability target the team aims for"],["SLA","Contractual promise to customers, often with penalties"],["p99 latency","99% of requests finish faster than this"],["Freshness target","How stale served data is allowed to be"]], why:"The SLO is set tighter than the SLA so you notice trouble before you owe customers."},
 ["Why set the latency target on p99 instead of the average?",["Averages hide the slow tail that real users hit","The average is noisier, so alerts on it fire too often","p99 is cheaper to compute than a mean on a stream","p99 stays flat under load, so it makes a steadier SLO"],"A 50 ms average can hide 1% of requests taking 2 s, and a heavy user making 100 calls per page hits that tail on most pages."]]],
["Latency Numbers Every Engineer Should Know",
"Orders of magnitude decide designs before any benchmark does:\n\n$main memory read       ~100 ns\nSSD random read        ~100 µs\nsame-datacenter trip   ~0.5 ms\nHDD seek               ~10 ms\ncross-continent trip   ~100–150 ms\n\nMemory is about 1000× faster than an SSD random read, so hot lookups belong in RAM (Redis or an in-process cache). Sequential reads beat random reads by a wide margin, which is why logs and columnar files scan fast.\n\nA cross-region call can’t fit inside a 50 ms budget, and hops add up: a request that makes ten sequential same-datacenter calls spends about 5 ms on the network before doing any work.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order these from fastest to slowest", items:["Main memory read","SSD random read","Round trip within a datacenter","Cross-continent round trip"], why:"Roughly 100 ns, 100 µs, 0.5 ms and 100+ ms."},
 ["A ranking service has a 50 ms budget. Can it call a feature service on another continent for every request?",["No, one round trip alone takes about 100–150 ms","Yes, if the remote service answers in under 10 ms","Yes, if the call reuses a warm HTTP/2 connection","Only at p50; it would just miss the p99 target"],"The speed of light sets a floor that no server speed-up or connection reuse removes."],
 ["Roughly how much faster is a RAM read than an SSD random read?",["About 1000×","About 10×","About 100×","About 1,000,000×"],"About 100 ns versus about 100 µs is a factor of 1000."]]],
["Estimating ML and LLM Workloads",
"Convert traffic into compute. For classic ML, multiply QPS by the items scored per request: ranking 500 candidates at 10k QPS means 5M model scores per second.\n\nFor LLMs, think in tokens. Weights alone take params × bytes per param:\n\n$70B params × 2 bytes (FP16) = 140 GB → more than one 80 GB GPU\n\nCost = requests × (input + output tokens) × price per token. Output tokens are priced several times higher than input and are generated one at a time, so they dominate latency. Input is cheaper per token, but RAG prompts are long: 8k tokens of retrieved context on every request can become the biggest line on the bill.",
[["How much memory do the FP16 weights of a 70B model take?",["About 140 GB","About 70 GB","About 35 GB","About 280 GB"],"Two bytes per parameter: 70B × 2 = 140 GB, already more than one 80 GB GPU before the KV cache. 70 GB is INT8, 35 GB INT4, 280 GB FP32."],
 {type:"gap", p:"A ranker scores 500 candidates per request at 2,000 QPS, so it must run about ___ model scores per second.", opts:["1 million","2,500","100,000","10 million"], why:"2,000 × 500 = 1,000,000."},
 ["Why do output tokens dominate LLM response time?",["Decoding emits one token per forward pass","Output tokens are larger than input tokens","Output tokens can’t be served from a cache","The prompt is processed after the output starts"],"Prefill handles the whole prompt in parallel; decoding is sequential, one forward pass per token."]]],
["APIs and Data Models in a Design Round",
"Before drawing boxes, define 2–4 key endpoints and the core entities. It pins down scope and makes the data flow obvious.\n\n$POST /v1/predict {user_id, context}\n  → {items:[{id, score}], model_version, request_id}\nGET  /v1/feed?cursor=abc&limit=20\n\nUse cursor-based pagination for feeds: offsets skip or repeat items when new posts arrive. Give writes an idempotency key so a client can retry a timed-out payment without charging twice.\n\nFor ML, the prediction contract matters. Log the model version and the exact features with every prediction so you can debug it and train on it later. For LLM output, stream tokens over SSE instead of waiting for the full response.",
[["Why use cursor-based pagination for a feed?",["New posts shift offsets, so pages skip or repeat items","Cursors make each page response smaller","Offset queries can’t be cached by a CDN or the browser","REST APIs don’t allow an offset parameter"],"A cursor anchors to an item, so new inserts don’t shift the page."],
 ["A client’s `POST /charge` times out. What makes retrying safe?",["A client-generated key the server dedupes on","Raising the client timeout to 60 seconds","Retrying with exponential backoff and jitter","Sending the retry to a different replica"],"The server sees the same idempotency key and returns the first result instead of charging again. Backoff spaces retries out but doesn’t stop a double charge."],
 ["What should a prediction service log with every response?",["The model version and the features it used","The latency and status code of each request","The request ID and the top-scored item only","The raw request body, without the scores"],"Version plus exact features makes predictions debuggable and reusable as training data."]]],
["Trade-offs and Leveling Signals",
"Strong candidates don’t just pick; they name the alternative and why it lost. ‘I’ll use a two-tower retriever rather than scoring the full catalog, because 100M items × 10k QPS is a trillion scores a second.’\n\nStart simple, then scale: a baseline that ships beats a diagram that can’t. Raise failure modes, monitoring and rollback before you’re asked.\n\nThe level is decided in the deep dives, not the first diagram. A senior signal is connecting follow-ups: position bias, cold start and feedback loops are one problem (the model only learns from what it showed) with one family of fixes (exploration and debiasing).",
[["What usually separates an E5 from an E6 outcome?",["Depth in the follow-up deep dives","A cleaner, more complete first diagram","Naming more specific technologies and vendors","Finishing the estimation section faster"],"Most candidates draw a similar first diagram; follow-ups reveal depth."],
 ["What’s the strongest way to justify a design choice?",["Name what you rejected and why it loses here","Point out that most big companies use it","Pick the tool with the best published benchmark","Say it’s the option you know how to operate"],"Trade-off reasoning against these requirements is what the rubric scores."],
 ["Which answer shows senior-level thinking about a recommender?",["Treating position bias, cold start and feedback loops as one exposure problem","Swapping the GBDT ranker for a larger transformer to lift offline NDCG","Adding dozens of new user and item features to the ranking model","Retraining the ranker hourly instead of daily so it stays fresh"],"The model only learns from what it showed, so all three share one cause and one family of fixes (exploration and debiasing)."]]]
]},

{t:"Scaling the Request Path", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Cache Write Strategies and Invalidation",
"How writes touch the cache sets both freshness and risk:\n\n$write-through: app → cache + DB      (fresh, slower writes)\nwrite-back:    app → cache → DB later (fast, can lose data)\nwrite-around:  app → DB only         (cache filled on read)\n\nWrite-back loses acknowledged writes if a cache node dies before flushing. Write-around stops one-off writes from evicting hot keys.\n\nWith cache-aside, delete the key on write instead of updating it. Two concurrent updates can land out of order and leave a stale value until expiry; a delete just forces a fresh read. TTLs are the simplest staleness bound.\n\nHit rate drives DB load: going from 95% to 99% cuts misses from 5% to 1%, a 5× drop.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each caching approach to its trade-off", pairs:[["Write-through","Always fresh, but every write pays for cache and DB"],["Write-back","Fastest writes, but data is lost if the cache node dies"],["Write-around","Writes skip the cache, so one-off writes don’t evict hot keys"],["TTL expiry","Bounds staleness without tracking individual writes"]], why:"Each strategy trades write latency, durability and freshness differently."},
 ["Cache hit rate rises from 95% to 99%. What happens to database reads from misses?",["They fall about 5×","They fall about 4%","They fall about 4×","They fall about 1.04×"],"Misses drop from 5% of requests to 1%, a 5× drop."],
 ["With concurrent writers, why delete the cache key instead of updating it?",["Racing updates can land out of order","A delete is cheaper than a write in Redis","Updating would need write-through mode","Deleting resets the key’s TTL to zero"],"An out-of-order update leaves a stale value until expiry; a delete makes the next reader fetch the database’s current value."]]],
["Timeouts, Retries and Circuit Breakers",
"Every remote call needs a timeout, or one slow dependency ties up every thread you have.\n\nRetry with exponential backoff plus jitter (about 100, 200, 400 ms, each randomized). Without jitter, thousands of clients retry in lockstep and flatten a recovering service. Only retry idempotent operations, or send an idempotency key.\n\nRetries multiply across layers: 3 tries at each of 3 layers turns one failing request into 27 calls at the bottom. Retry budgets (say, retries capped at 10% of traffic) prevent this.\n\nA circuit breaker trips after repeated failures and fails fast instead of waiting, then lets a few probes through to test recovery. Pair it with a fallback: a cached result, a simpler model, or a popular-items list.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order a circuit breaker’s states as a dependency fails and recovers", items:["Closed: calls flow normally","Open: calls fail fast once errors cross a threshold","Half-open: a few probe calls test recovery","Closed again after the probes succeed"], why:"Failing fast while open protects both the caller and the struggling dependency."},
 ["Why add jitter to exponential backoff?",["So clients don’t retry in synchronized waves","So each retry waits less than the one before","So retries go to a different replica each time","So the total number of retries is capped"],"Randomized delays spread the retry load over time. Capping retries is the job of a retry budget."],
 ["The ranking model times out on a feed request. What’s the best fallback?",["Serve a cached or popularity-based list","Retry the ranker with a longer timeout","Return an error so the client can retry","Block until a ranker replica responds"],"A slightly worse feed beats an empty screen, and extra retries pile load on a struggling service."]]],
["Real-Time Delivery: Polling, WebSockets, SSE",
"Three ways to push updates to clients:\n\n$long polling: client asks, server holds until data, client asks again\nSSE:          one HTTP response, server streams events one way\nWebSocket:    full-duplex channel, either side sends any time\n\nLong polling is simple and works everywhere. Server-Sent Events are the standard for streaming LLM tokens: plain HTTP, one direction, built-in reconnect. WebSockets suit chat, multiplayer and live collaboration, where the client also sends often.\n\nPersistent connections complicate scaling. Each server holds thousands of open sockets, so you need a connection gateway or sticky routing, plus graceful draining on deploys.\n\nStreaming improves perceived latency: users notice time to first token (TTFT), not the 8 s total.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each use case to the best fit", pairs:[["Streaming LLM tokens to a browser","Server-Sent Events"],["Multiplayer game state","WebSockets"],["Occasional updates with minimal infrastructure","Long polling"],["Static images and scripts","Plain HTTP through a CDN"]], why:"Pick the simplest transport that matches the direction and frequency of messages."},
 ["Why does streaming make an LLM feel faster even when the total time is the same?",["Text appears almost at once","Streaming generates fewer tokens","Streaming lets the server batch more","The model skips the prefill step"],"Perceived latency is driven by time to first token, not total generation time."],
 ["What makes WebSocket servers harder to scale than stateless HTTP servers?",["Hour-long connections pin clients to servers","WebSocket frames can’t be sent over TLS","Each message needs a new TCP handshake","Load balancers can’t forward the upgrade"],"You can’t freely rebalance an open connection, so you need a gateway or sticky routing plus draining on deploys."]]]
]},

{t:"Storage, Partitioning & Consistency", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Consistency Models and PACELC",
"Consistency is a spectrum. Strong: every read sees the latest write. Session guarantees: read-your-writes, and monotonic reads (you never go back in time). Eventual: replicas converge later.\n\nPACELC extends CAP: during a Partition choose Availability or Consistency; Else, even when healthy, choose Latency or Consistency. Synchronous replication costs latency on every write.\n\nDynamo-style stores use quorums. With N replicas, writes wait for W acks and reads query R replicas:\n\n$N=3, W=2, R=2 → R + W = 4 > 3 → every read set overlaps the latest write\n\nEventual consistency is fine for feeds, like counts and most feature stores. It’s not fine for account balances.",
[{type:"gap", p:"With N replicas, quorum reads are guaranteed to overlap the latest write when R + W ___ N.", opts:["is greater than","equals","is less than","is at least half of"], why:"Overlapping read and write sets share at least one up-to-date replica, which needs R + W > N."},
 ["PACELC’s ‘Else’ clause says that without a partition…",["You still trade latency against consistency","You can have consistency and availability together","Replicas converge, so consistency is automatic","You choose between availability and durability"],"Waiting for more replicas to confirm makes every operation slower, even when the network is healthy."],
 ["Which data can safely be eventually consistent?",["A ‘clicks in the last hour’ feature","A user’s account balance","The last unit of stock for a product","A unique username at sign-up"],"A slightly stale feature barely moves a prediction; stale balances, stock or usernames lead to overspending, overselling or duplicates."]]],
["Object Storage and Blob Data",
"Object stores (S3, GCS, Azure Blob) hold huge, cheap, durable blobs: training datasets, model checkpoints, images, video and raw documents for RAG. S3 is designed for 11 nines of durability and costs around $0.02 per GB-month.\n\nThe catch is per-request latency, often tens of milliseconds to the first byte, so object stores suit bulk reads, not hot lookups. Keep metadata in a database row that points to the blob key:\n\n$client → API: request upload → presigned URL\nclient → object store: bytes go direct\nAPI → DB: {id, owner, key, size}\n\nPresigned URLs let clients upload large files straight to storage, so your app servers never carry the bytes.",
[["Why not serve per-request feature lookups from S3?",["Time to first byte is tens of milliseconds","S3 durability is too low for features","S3 can’t store objects under 1 MB","S3 requests can’t be made in parallel"],"Hot lookups belong in a cache or an online key-value store; S3 suits bulk reads."],
 {type:"order", p:"Order a direct-to-storage upload", items:["Client asks the API to upload a file","API returns a presigned URL","Client uploads the bytes straight to object storage","API records the metadata and object key in the database"], why:"The app server handles a small request; the heavy bytes skip it entirely."},
 ["Where should a 400 GB training checkpoint and its metadata live?",["Blob in object storage, metadata in a database","Both in Postgres, with the blob as a bytea column","Both in object storage, metadata as a JSON sidecar","Blob on the trainer’s local SSD, metadata in Redis"],"Blobs go where storage is cheap and durable; queryable metadata goes where queries are fast. A local SSD dies with the machine."]]],
["Search Engines and Inverted Indexes",
"An inverted index maps each term to a posting list of the documents that contain it:\n\n$‘timeout’ → [doc3, doc9, doc41]\n‘E1043’   → [doc9]\n\nA query merges posting lists, then scores matches with BM25. It rewards term frequency with saturation (the tenth mention adds little) and weights rare terms higher through inverse document frequency.\n\nLexical search catches exact matches that embeddings miss: product IDs, error codes, names. That’s why it is the lexical half of hybrid RAG and the retrieval stage of classic search (Elasticsearch, OpenSearch).\n\nAt scale, indexes are sharded by document. A query is scattered to every shard, each returns its top-k, and a coordinator gathers and merges them.",
[["What does an inverted index map?",["Each term to the documents that contain it","Each document to the list of terms it contains","Each document to its embedding vector","Each query to the results it returned"],"Lookups go from a query word straight to matching documents. Document → terms is the forward index."],
 ["Why does BM25 saturate term frequency?",["So repeating a word 50 times stops adding much","So common words get a higher weight","So long documents always rank first","So rare terms are ignored at query time"],"After a few mentions, more repeats say little about relevance. Rare terms are boosted by IDF, not ignored."],
 ["A user searches for the error code ‘ERR_4021’. Which retriever is most reliable?",["BM25 keyword search","Dense embeddings alone","A cross-encoder over random docs","Popularity-based ranking"],"Exact rare tokens are BM25’s strength and an embedding model’s weak spot."]]]
]},

{t:"Async Messaging & Data Processing", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Kafka: Logs, Partitions, Consumer Groups",
"Kafka is a durable, partitioned, replayable log. Producers append records to a topic’s partitions, and records stay for the retention period even after being read.\n\n$topic clicks: P0 | P1 | P2 | P3\ngroup A: c1 ← P0, P1    c2 ← P2, P3\n\nOrder is guaranteed only within a partition, so key records by entity ID (such as `user_id`) to keep each user’s events in order. Within a consumer group each partition goes to one consumer, so parallelism is capped at the partition count: a fifth consumer on four partitions sits idle.\n\nRetention enables replay, such as rebuilding training data from last month’s events. Watch consumer lag, the gap between the newest offset and the committed one.",
[["A topic has 8 partitions and one group runs 12 consumers. How many consumers are active?",["8","12","4","6"],"Each partition goes to at most one consumer in a group, so 4 of the 12 sit idle."],
 ["How do you keep each user’s events in order?",["Use `user_id` as the partition key","Use a random key to balance partitions","Give each user their own consumer group","Increase the topic’s retention period"],"All records with the same key land in the same partition, which is ordered. A random key spreads one user across partitions."],
 {type:"gap", p:"The key health metric for a Kafka consumer is consumer ___, the gap between the latest offset and the committed offset.", opts:["lag","skew","drift","throughput"], why:"Growing lag means consumers can’t keep up and downstream data is going stale."}]],
["Delivery Semantics and Idempotency",
"At-most-once: commit the offset, then process; a crash loses the message. At-least-once: process, then commit; a crash means redelivery and a duplicate. ‘Exactly-once’ in practice is at-least-once delivery plus idempotent or transactional processing.\n\nDedupe with a unique event ID: record processed IDs and skip repeats. The outbox pattern fixes the dual write, where the DB update succeeds but the event publish fails:\n\n$one DB transaction: update order + insert outbox row\nrelay: read outbox → publish to Kafka → mark sent\n\nEnd-to-end exactly-once needs the sink to cooperate with idempotent upserts or transactions. It matters for ML: duplicated click events double-count positives and bias labels.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each delivery approach to its behavior", pairs:[["At-most-once","May lose messages, never duplicates them"],["At-least-once","Never loses messages, may duplicate them"],["Effectively exactly-once","At-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing"],["Outbox pattern","DB change and event saved in one transaction"]], why:"Real systems pick at-least-once and make duplicates harmless."},
 ["A service updates the database, then publishes an event, and sometimes crashes in between. What fixes it?",["An outbox row written in the same DB transaction","Publishing the event first, then writing the database","Retrying the publish until Kafka acknowledges it","Using a consumer group with exactly-once enabled"],"A relay publishes from the outbox, so the event can’t be lost once the row commits. Retries don’t help if the process has already crashed."],
 ["Why do duplicate click events hurt a model?",["They double-count positives and bias labels","They make training jobs run twice as long","They force the feature store to resize","They only matter if the events are out of order"],"The model learns that duplicated items are more clickable than they are."]]],
["Batch Processing (Spark)",
"Batch jobs (Spark, BigQuery, Beam in batch mode) process bounded datasets on a schedule: nightly feature tables, training-set generation, batch scoring of every user. They’re cheap per record and easy to reason about, but results are hours stale.\n\nPartition tables by date so each run reads only new partitions (`dt=2026-10-21`) instead of rescanning history. When feature logic changes, run a backfill that recomputes past partitions with the new code, so training data stays consistent.\n\nThe expensive part is the shuffle: joins and group-bys move data across the network between workers. Skewed keys (one giant user) create straggler tasks; salting the key or broadcasting a small table helps.",
[["The most expensive step in a typical Spark job is…",["The shuffle behind joins and group-bys","Map-side filters applied on every partition","Scheduling tasks onto executors","Reading Parquet column footers"],"Shuffles move data across the network between workers and spill to disk."],
 ["You fix a bug in a feature’s logic. How do you keep training data consistent?",["Backfill past partitions with the new code","Apply the fix only to partitions from today on","Drop the feature until enough new data exists","Keep the old logic in training, new in serving"],"Otherwise old and new rows follow different definitions; the last option causes training-serving skew."],
 ["What is the main downside of batch scoring?",["Predictions are hours stale","Cost per record is high","Backfills become impossible","It needs GPUs to run"],"Anything that happened since the last run isn’t reflected; per-record cost is actually low."]]],
["Stream Processing: Windows and Watermarks",
"Stream processors (Flink, Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka Streams) compute over unbounded data in windows:\n\n$tumbling: fixed, non-overlapping (every 5 min)\nsliding:  overlapping (last 5 min, advancing every 1 min)\nsession:  closes after a gap of inactivity\n\nEvent time (when it happened) differs from processing time (when it arrived); a phone that was offline sends old events late. A watermark declares ‘events up to time T have probably arrived’. Waiting longer catches more late events but delays results.\n\nLambda architecture runs batch and stream side by side and merges them, with two codebases. Kappa uses only the stream and reprocesses by replaying the log. Streaming fits features like ‘clicks in the last 5 minutes’.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each streaming term to its meaning", pairs:[["Tumbling window","Fixed, non-overlapping intervals"],["Sliding window","Overlapping intervals that advance by a step"],["Session window","Closes after a gap of inactivity"],["Watermark","How far event time is believed to be complete"]], why:"Windows define what to aggregate; watermarks define when to emit."},
 ["You raise the watermark delay from 10 seconds to 5 minutes. What happens?",["More late events count, but results arrive later","Results arrive sooner and include more late events","Fewer late events count, but results arrive sooner","Window sizes grow from 10 seconds to 5 minutes"],"Watermarks trade completeness against latency; window size is a separate setting."],
 ["How does a Kappa architecture recompute after a logic change?",["It replays the retained log","It reruns a separate batch layer","It restores a database snapshot","It recomputes only new events"],"One codebase, with the retained log as the source of truth. A batch layer is Lambda."]]],
["Workflow Orchestration",
"DAG orchestrators (Airflow, Dagster, Kubeflow Pipelines) run pipelines as directed acyclic graphs: each task starts after its dependencies finish, with retries, alerts and backfills.\n\n$wait_for_logs → build_features → train → evaluate → register\n\nTasks must be idempotent, for example overwriting a dated partition instead of appending, so a retry or backfill doesn’t duplicate data. Sensors wait for upstream data, such as yesterday’s partition landing, instead of guessing a start time.\n\nDurable-execution engines (Temporal) suit long, stateful workflows. Each step’s result is persisted, so after a crash the workflow resumes where it stopped instead of restarting. That fits multi-day jobs, human approvals and long agent runs.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order the tasks in a daily training DAG", items:["Sensor waits for yesterday’s logs","Build features","Train the model","Evaluate against the production model","Register the model if it wins"], why:"Each task depends on the output of the one before it."},
 ["Why should a DAG task overwrite its date partition rather than append to it?",["So retries and backfills don’t duplicate data","So downstream tasks can start reading earlier","So the orchestrator can skip the sensor step","So partitions stay small enough to cache"],"An idempotent task produces the same result however many times it runs."],
 ["An agent run takes hours and calls many tools. The worker crashes halfway. What avoids starting over?",["A durable-execution engine like Temporal","An Airflow DAG with task retries enabled","A cron job that restarts the run hourly","A larger worker with more memory"],"Persisted step results let the workflow resume from the last completed step. A retried task still starts that task from scratch."]]],
["Fan-out on Write vs Fan-out on Read",
"A news feed can be assembled two ways:\n\n$push (on write): post → copy into each follower’s inbox → read is one lookup\npull (on read):  read → fetch each followee’s recent posts → merge\n\nPush makes reads fast, but a celebrity with 50M followers turns one post into 50M inbox writes. Pull keeps writes cheap, but every feed load fans out to hundreds of followees.\n\nHybrids push for normal accounts and pull high-follower accounts at read time, merging the two. That merged set is what ML ranking needs: it becomes the candidate pool a ranker orders by predicted engagement.",
[["What is the main problem with pure fan-out on write?",["One celebrity post means millions of writes","Every feed read fans out to many followees","Inboxes can’t be cached in memory","Posts reach followers in random order"],"Write cost grows with follower count; slow fan-out reads are the pull model’s problem."],
 ["How do hybrid feeds handle celebrity accounts?",["Pull their posts at read time and merge","Push their posts to active followers only","Push them in batches over a few hours","Store them in a separate celebrity feed"],"Most users get cheap reads; a few huge accounts avoid write storms."],
 {type:"gap", p:"In an ML-ranked feed, the merged posts from push and pull become the ___ set that the ranker scores.", opts:["candidate","training","holdout","feature"], why:"Feed assembly is candidate generation; ranking comes next."}]]
]},

{t:"The ML Design Framework: Problem, Labels, Metrics", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Framing: Business Goal to ML Task",
"‘Increase engagement’ isn’t a model. Turn it into a prediction task whose output fits the product surface:\n\n$will the user click?       → binary classification\nhow long will they watch?  → regression\nwhat order to show?        → ranking\nwhich 500 of 10M items?    → retrieval\n\nName the label and objective before the architecture, and write the exact label definition, such as ‘a click followed by at least 10 s of dwell within an hour’.\n\nFirst ask whether ML is needed at all; a rule or a popularity sort may do. Then watch the proxy: optimizing clicks can reward clickbait and hurt satisfaction, the real goal.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each product question to its ML task", pairs:[["Is this transaction fraud?","Binary classification"],["How many minutes will they watch?","Regression"],["In what order should results appear?","Ranking"],["Which few hundred of millions of items to consider?","Retrieval"]], why:"The output type follows from what the product surface needs."},
 ["What should you state before choosing a model architecture?",["The label and the objective","The serving hardware and GPU type","The feature store and its schema","The embedding size and layer count"],"The architecture only makes sense once you know what it predicts."],
 ["A model optimized for clicks raises CTR, but satisfaction surveys drop. Why?",["Clicks are a proxy that rewards clickbait","The model is underfitting the click data","CTR is miscalibrated at serving time","Surveys lag the model by a few weeks"],"Optimizing a proxy can diverge from the goal it stands in for."]]],
["Choosing Labels: Explicit, Implicit, Delayed",
"Explicit labels (ratings, reports, thumbs-down) are clean but sparse. Implicit labels (clicks, dwell time, purchases) are plentiful but noisy and biased by what was shown. Clean implicit labels with thresholds: a click with under 5 s of dwell is probably a misclick.\n\nNegatives are usually impressions without engagement, but an item below the fold that was never seen isn’t a true negative.\n\nSome labels arrive late: chargebacks land 60+ days after a purchase. You either wait (stale training data) or train on partial labels and correct for them.\n\nHuman labels cost money and need written guidelines. Every label definition encodes business priorities, so choose it deliberately.",
[["How do you separate real clicks from misclicks?",["Require a minimum dwell time","Count only clicks on the top result","Use only explicit star ratings","Drop users who click very often"],"A quick bounce back suggests the click wasn’t wanted. Ratings are clean but too sparse."],
 ["Fraud labels arrive with 60-day chargebacks. What is the trade-off?",["Stale mature labels vs noisy partial ones","Precision vs recall at the decision threshold","Model size vs scoring latency at checkout","Label cost vs the number of annotators"],"Recent data is the most relevant but the least labeled."],
 ["What is a common source of negative labels in a feed?",["Seen impressions without engagement","Items that were never shown to the user","Items scrolled past too fast to register","Random items sampled from the catalog"],"Unshown or never-seen items carry no signal about the user’s preference. Random negatives are used for retrieval, but they’re weak negatives for a ranker."]]],
["Choosing Offline Metrics",
"Match the metric to the task:\n\n$classification: precision, recall, PR-AUC, ROC-AUC, log loss\nranking:        NDCG, MAP, recall@k, MRR\nads / auctions: calibration, normalized entropy\n\nUnder heavy imbalance (0.1% fraud), ROC-AUC looks great because true negatives dominate; PR-AUC shows how many flagged cases are really fraud. NDCG discounts gains by position, so relevant items at the top count most.\n\nWhen scores feed an auction or a threshold, calibration matters: predicted CTR / actual CTR should be about 1. Normalized entropy is log loss divided by the log loss of always predicting the base rate; below 1 means the model beats that baseline.",
[["Fraud is 0.1% of transactions. Which metric is most informative?",["PR-AUC","Accuracy","ROC-AUC alone","Log loss alone"],"PR-AUC focuses on the rare positive class; accuracy is 99.9% for a model that flags nothing, and ROC-AUC is flattered by true negatives."],
 {type:"match", p:"Match each metric to what it measures", pairs:[["NDCG","Ranking quality, rewarding relevant items near the top"],["Calibration ratio","Predicted CTR divided by actual CTR"],["Normalized entropy","Log loss relative to always predicting the base rate"],["Recall@k","Share of relevant items found in the top k"]], why:"Ranking, probability quality and coverage each need their own metric."},
 ["Why does an ads model need calibration, not just good ranking?",["Predicted CTR sets the price in the auction","Calibrated models train faster and cheaper","NDCG is undefined for ads click data","Uncalibrated scores break the ad index"],"A model that ranks well but overpredicts CTR still misprices every ad."]]],
["North-Star, Guardrail and Counter Metrics",
"Pick one primary metric that defines success, such as bookings per user. Add guardrails that can veto a launch even when the primary metric wins: p99 latency, serving cost, crash rate, reports, unsubscribes. In 2026 rounds, latency and cost per request are expected guardrails.\n\nCounter-metrics catch gaming. Clickbait raises CTR while dwell time drops and ‘hide’ rates rise.\n\nShort-term engagement is easy to inflate; long-term metrics like 28-day retention resist it. Define every metric before the experiment starts, or you’ll be tempted to report whichever one moved.",
[["A new ranker lifts CTR 3%, but p99 latency rises from 180 ms to 400 ms and breaches the guardrail. What do you do?",["Hold the launch until latency is fixed","Launch now, and fix latency in a follow-up","Launch to 50% so the latency hit is halved","Re-run the test until the guardrail passes"],"Guardrails exist precisely to veto wins that hurt the product elsewhere; rerunning until it passes is p-hacking."],
 {type:"gap", p:"Clickbait raises CTR but lowers dwell time, so dwell time acts as a ___ metric.", opts:["counter","north-star","vanity","primary"], why:"A counter-metric moves the wrong way when the primary metric is being gamed."},
 ["Why define metrics before the experiment starts?",["So you can’t cherry-pick whichever one moved","So the test can be shortened once it’s significant","So the dashboards are ready on launch day","So guardrails can be dropped if they lag"],"Testing many metrics after the fact finds false wins by chance."]]],
["Baselines and Model Progression",
"Climb the ladder only when data and gains justify it:\n\n$heuristic (popularity, rules) → logistic regression / GBDT → deep model\n\nThe heuristic sets the bar and stays as the fallback when serving fails. GBDT (XGBoost, LightGBM) is the strong default on tabular features: little preprocessing, fast on CPU, hard to beat.\n\nDeep models win when inputs include huge sparse IDs (users, items) that need learned embeddings, or multimodal content like text and images. Airbnb’s search team moved from GBDT to neural networks only after GBDT gains plateaued, and found the switch harder than expected. Each step adds serving cost and debugging pain, so say why it’s worth it.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order the typical model progression", items:["Popularity or rule-based heuristic","Logistic regression or GBDT","Deep model with learned embeddings"], why:"Add complexity only once the simpler model’s gains plateau."},
 ["When do deep models clearly beat GBDT?",["Huge sparse IDs, or text and images","Small, clean tabular datasets","Strict CPU-only latency budgets","Few features with simple interactions"],"Embeddings let deep models learn from millions of IDs and raw content; GBDT stays the strong default on tabular data."],
 ["What role does the heuristic baseline play after launch?",["It’s the fallback when the model fails","It’s deleted once the model ships to everyone","It replaces the A/B test control","It labels the next training set"],"A popularity list keeps the product working during outages and sets the bar new models must beat."]]],
["Splits, Sampling and Leakage",
"Split by time for anything temporal: train on weeks 1–8, test on week 9. Random splits let the model peek at the future and inflate offline scores. Use group splits so one user never appears in both train and test.\n\nLeakage is a feature that contains the label or future information. ‘Refund issued’ predicts fraud perfectly offline and is useless at checkout, when it doesn’t exist yet.\n\nFor imbalance, downsample negatives or use class weights. Downsampling inflates predicted probabilities, so recalibrate:\n\n$q = p / (p + (1 − p) / w)\n\nwhere p is the model’s output, w the fraction of negatives kept, and q the corrected probability.",
[["Why do random splits on time-ordered data mislead?",["The model trains on the future","The test set ends up too small","Classes become imbalanced","Training takes much longer"],"Production always predicts forward in time, so evaluation should too; peeking at the future inflates offline scores."],
 ["Which feature leaks the label when predicting fraud at checkout?",["‘Refund issued’","Card country","Transaction amount","Minutes since the account was created"],"It only exists after fraud has been detected."],
 {type:"gap", p:"After downsampling negatives, predicted probabilities come out too high, so you must ___ them.", opts:["recalibrate","normalize to sum to one","clip at 0.5","upsample"], why:"The model learned a base rate that’s higher than reality; q = p / (p + (1 − p) / w) corrects it."}]]
]},

{t:"Data & Feature Platforms", tr:"sd", ls:[
["Lakes, Warehouses and Lakehouses",
"A data lake is object storage plus open files like Parquet: cheap, fine for raw logs and unstructured data, with schema applied on read. A warehouse (BigQuery, Snowflake) gives fast SQL over modeled tables and powers BI. A lakehouse (Delta Lake, Iceberg, Hudi) adds ACID transactions, schema enforcement and time travel on top of lake files.\n\nParquet is columnar: a training job reading 20 of 500 columns scans only those 20. Partition by date so jobs skip old data.\n\nTime travel matters for ML. Querying a table `VERSION AS OF` a past snapshot rebuilds the exact data a model trained on, making training sets reproducible.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each option to its strength", pairs:[["Data lake","Cheap storage for raw and unstructured data"],["Warehouse","Fast SQL over modeled data for BI"],["Lakehouse","ACID tables and time travel on lake files"],["Parquet","Columnar files that read only the needed columns"]], why:"Most ML platforms keep raw data in a lake or lakehouse and reporting in a warehouse."},
 ["How does time travel help ML teams?",["It rebuilds the exact data a model trained on","It keeps hot partitions in memory for faster reads","It lets late events overwrite past partitions","It lets you query rows before they’re validated"],"Reproducing a training set is key to debugging and audits."],
 ["A training job reads 20 of 500 columns. Why is Parquet a good fit?",["Its columnar layout skips the other 480","It compresses rows as gzipped JSON lines","It indexes every column like a B-tree","It loads the whole file into memory once"],"Scanning 4% of the columns means far less I/O."]]],
["Data Quality, Contracts and Lineage",
"Upstream changes break models silently: a column renamed, prices switched from dollars to cents, a field suddenly 40% null. Nothing crashes; predictions just get worse.\n\nData contracts are agreed schemas and expectations between producers and consumers. Validation checks (Great Expectations, TFX Data Validation) gate pipelines on null rates, ranges and distributions, at ingestion and again before training.\n\nVersion data together with code and config so any model can be rebuilt. Lineage records which tables fed which features and models, so when a bad upstream change lands you can list every affected model and retrain or roll back.",
[["An upstream team switches prices from dollars to cents. What catches it before training?",["A range check in data validation","A schema check on column names and types","Monitoring the model’s online CTR","A unit test in the training code"],"Values suddenly 100× larger fail an expected-range check; the column name and type didn’t change, so a schema check passes."],
 ["What does lineage let you do after a bad upstream change?",["List every feature and model built on that table","Roll the source table back to yesterday’s snapshot","Block the upstream team from changing the schema","Re-run validation checks on the affected column"],"You can’t fix what you can’t trace. Snapshots and contracts are separate tools."],
 ["Where should data validation run?",["At ingestion and again before training","Only at ingestion, once per batch","Only on online predictions after deploy","Only inside the training notebook"],"Catch bad data early, and again before it shapes a model."]]],
["Labeling Pipelines",
"Labels come from several sources, mixed by cost and quality:\n\n$human annotators: guidelines + gold questions + agreement checks\nweak supervision: heuristic rules vote on labels\nactive learning:  label what the model is least sure about\nLLM-assisted:     cheap draft labels, audited by humans\n\nMeasure inter-annotator agreement with Cohen’s kappa. Low agreement usually means the guideline is unclear, not that raters are careless. Hide gold questions with known answers in the queue to catch careless raters.\n\nUse multiple raters for subjective tasks like content policy. LLM labels cost a fraction of human ones, but spot-check them against a gold set before trusting them.",
[{type:"match", p:"Match each labeling technique to its purpose", pairs:[["Cohen’s kappa","Measures agreement between raters beyond chance"],["Gold questions","Known-answer items that catch careless raters"],["Active learning","Spends the budget on examples the model is unsure about"],["Weak supervision","Combines noisy heuristic rules into labels"]], why:"Each tool improves either label quality or labeling efficiency."},
 ["Before trusting LLM-generated labels, you should…",["Check them against a human-labeled gold set","Use a larger LLM and accept its labels","Average labels from three prompts","Keep only labels the LLM is confident in"],"LLM labelers can be systematically, confidently wrong in ways only an audit reveals."],
 ["Annotators often disagree on a policy task. What’s the most likely fix?",["Clarify the guidelines; use several raters","Replace the raters with the lowest kappa","Pay more per item so raters slow down","Keep only items everyone agreed on"],"Disagreement usually signals an ambiguous definition, not careless raters."]]],
["Features at Scale",
"Feature families: user (account age, past CTR), item (category, price, freshness), context (time, device, query) and cross features (user × item, like ‘has this user bought from this seller?’).\n\nHigh-cardinality IDs with millions of values become learned embeddings or hashed buckets. `hash(id) % 2^20` bounds memory but lets unrelated IDs collide.\n\nCounts and rates are the workhorses, such as ‘item CTR over 7 days’. Smooth rates at low counts, or 1 click from 1 view looks like 100% CTR:\n\n$smoothed_ctr = (clicks + α) / (views + α + β)\n\nCross features capture interactions explicitly, though GBDTs and deep networks can also learn them.",
[["An item has 1 click from 1 view. Why smooth its CTR?",["1 of 1 is too little data to trust","Rates over 50% break the model","Smoothing makes serving faster","Raw CTR can’t be stored as a float"],"The prior pulls sparse items toward a sensible average instead of a 100% CTR."],
 ["What is the cost of the hashing trick?",["Unrelated IDs can share a bucket","Memory grows with every new ID seen","Unseen IDs can’t be looked up","Lookups become O(log n) per ID"],"Bounded memory comes at the price of collisions; new IDs simply hash into an existing bucket."],
 ["A user ID column has 200M distinct values. What’s the best representation for a deep ranker?",["A learned embedding, maybe over hashed IDs","A 200M-wide one-hot vector per example","The raw integer ID as a numeric feature","The user’s ID bucketed into 10 ranges"],"Embeddings compress each ID into a small dense vector learned from data. A raw integer implies a meaningless order."]]],
["Online vs Offline Stores and Log-and-Wait",
"A feature store has two halves:\n\n$offline store (warehouse/lake): full history → training\nonline store (Redis, Cassandra, DynamoDB): latest value per key → serving\n\nThe online store is a low-latency key-value lookup: fetch 50 features for a user in a few milliseconds. Uber’s Michelangelo served predictions at p95 under 10 ms including Cassandra feature fetches, and shared about 10k features across teams.\n\nAn alternative to point-in-time joins is log-and-wait: log the exact features the model saw at serving time, then join labels when they arrive. Training data matches serving by construction, so there’s no skew. The cost: a new feature has training data only from the day you start logging it.",
[["Why is the online store a key-value database like Redis or Cassandra?",["Serving needs millisecond reads by key","It must hold the full feature history","It must run joins across feature tables","It must scan columns for training jobs"],"Serving needs fast point reads of the latest values, not analytics."],
 ["What is the main benefit of log-and-wait?",["No training-serving skew by construction","No storage needed for training data","New features get history instantly","Labels arrive faster than with joins"],"The model trains on exactly the features it saw in production."],
 ["What is the main drawback of log-and-wait for a new feature?",["It has no history until logs accumulate","It reintroduces training-serving skew","Logs can’t be joined with late labels","It needs a point-in-time join anyway"],"There’s no history to backfill from the serving logs."]]],
["Streaming and Real-Time Features",
"Some features go stale in seconds: ‘transactions on this card in the last minute’ for fraud, current traffic for ETA, clicks this session for feeds. Compute them as windowed aggregates in a stream processor and write them to the online store:\n\n$events → Kafka → Flink (1-min window per card) → online store → model\n\nStreaming features improve fraud recall against fast attacks, but they add infrastructure and on-call cost. Training must use the same windowing logic, or skew creeps in, and late events and watermark settings change the values too.\n\nDecide per feature: a user’s home city can refresh daily, while card velocity needs seconds.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order the path of a real-time fraud feature", items:["Payment event is published to Kafka","Stream processor aggregates a 1-minute window per card","Result is written to the online store","Model reads it when scoring the next payment"], why:"Events flow through the stream job into the store the model reads from."},
 ["Which feature most needs real-time updates?",["Transactions on a card in the last minute","Merchant’s average order value over 30 days","User’s count of chargebacks in the past year","Days since the card was first used"],"Card velocity changes within seconds during an attack; the others move slowly."],
 ["Training computes a ‘last hour’ count in a daily batch job, while serving uses Flink with different window rules. What’s the risk?",["Training-serving skew","Higher consumer lag","Label leakage","Data loss on crash"],"The model sees different values in production than it learned from."]]],
["Embedding Pipelines",
"Item, user and document embeddings are produced by batch or streaming jobs, stored with their model version, and indexed into an ANN store for retrieval.\n\nVectors from different embedding models live in different spaces, so you can’t mix them. A query embedded with v2 and compared against documents embedded with v1 returns noise. Upgrading the model is a backfill plus a cutover:\n\n$re-embed all items (v2) → build index v2 → shadow test → switch reads → retire v1\n\nNew or edited items flow through an incremental pipeline so they’re searchable within minutes. At 100M items a full re-embed is a big GPU job, so plan and budget for it.",
[{type:"order", p:"Order an embedding model upgrade", items:["Re-embed every item with the new model","Build a new ANN index","Shadow-test retrieval quality","Switch query traffic to the new index","Retire the old index"], why:"Keep the old index serving until the new one is proven."},
 ["Why can’t you embed queries with model v2 and search a v1 index?",["The two vector spaces are incompatible","v2 vectors always have a different dimension","ANN indexes are read-only once built","v1 vectors would need re-normalizing"],"Distances only mean something within one model’s space, even when the dimensions match."],
 ["What should be stored with every vector?",["The embedding model version","The vector’s L2 norm","The ANN shard it was assigned","The last query that retrieved it"],"The version tells you which vectors need re-embedding and which index they belong in."]]]
]}

);

